  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA weak surface cold front has slipped south of the CWA today,\nwith associated low/mid cloud cover across the higher terrain\nhaving dissipated, leading to widespread sunshine this afternoon \namid temperatures a few degrees cooler than that of yesterday. \nLows tonight will generally range from the low/mid 30s across \nthe mountains, while mid 30s (typical northern valley cold \nspots) to mid 40s across the lowlands. Some frost is expected \nacross the mountains, and potentially the northern lowlands, but\nwould be rather isolated and not necessitate a headline. Some \npatchy dense river valley fog is expected in the 'typical' \nlocations towards dawn.\n\nDry weather then continues through much of the weekend as\nsurface high pressure traverses north of the area, with\ntemperatures remaining in general ~ 5-10 degrees above normal,\nbut amid a decent amount of upper-level cloud cover. A weak, \ntraversing upper wave will bring the potential for showers \nSunday night into Monday, but overall, dry low-levels and \ndownsloping flow will result in showers (sprinkles) being of \nthe isolated variety, primarily confined to far western West \nVirginia, northeast Kentucky, and portions of southeast Ohio.\n\nTemperatures will warm back into the low/mid 70s Tuesday and\nWednesday given strengthening return flow ahead of the next\nstronger disturbance approaching from the central CONUS. This\ndisturbance and associated frontal boundary will bring a much\nhigher potential for the first area-wide rainfall in over two\nweeks to the region beginning later Wednesday and especially on\nThursday.",
